#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128882 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "1b13d9bb-9bcf-50be-a58d-39e82d99b9bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5530" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/5f3cd6aeb514ee0a59d1f3534342f85e1c00743d9e7c7004b8aaf8ccfbe353f9.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128883 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/id #uuid "26e641c9-bda5-5b93-8137-f4ec744ddb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5531" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/d1bc2b703c1d73e735bdbb1d2abab86f86447e87fbeabde57126114726f78757.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/558cbc7aba862d44e34a7b109ea84c1f7a6f3f1f4b505e5f476e62a29fdd654b.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\n- From the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ca2243ed8015927b9db5428d676ee59907264fc223721246a81b2e73aca3b351.png) we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/7a5bb57b71eb218271f2bb0ab718304d6b834738f1004eac7c501a7fd4ffa2b3.png) we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/0ea51255e7594236cdd5d8dee0997f64b49f77cbb77e8cfd97937830a862283b.png)\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ff8f2c8cace2b5c8c2c2ce2fcd0d5c24a4d1f4692e8e06464a9ae4666d429224.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "75f39ec3-0963-5555-b418-d5b2df3be0ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5532" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/74cc68e763183d4599dc223cce44849ace88129bbc82b6d49bcbafc4a1a8163d.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and that $A=PCP^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, find the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/b5c1766ea1d9eb7a913fd7be2d7bf71909c81da03aaff8f4c631e1a3955f9589.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/7f29c1c9be88268b14ef9131599420874fff6a399a3e3c92d3f4b635906163f6.png)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions to the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3-6\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 9 & -3-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3-6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+(-3-6\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3(1+2\\textrm{i})x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/ab958a638d0bce3cb2498d6f64016e581bb303fd0df725abcfaa58dbd836b95d.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=3$, we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/631f718ed2642536c5a8e02dedc38544af5e8b6cc48bff7a0afcf77c0d057d1b.png)\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/id #uuid "e0469305-b1d9-5430-9911-2f0c209b3264" :question/math-academy-id "e-8734" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/f4a051da70308d92a5530fca3ba9c214cff919a6072e0e4d863f5aad10f405ee.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071601/assets/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e905837-05cd-5930-b5d5-a8736fcd68df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372289} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1439c8-fdc4-5236-bf01-4122ff8ff749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372290} {:answer/id #uuid "dc53647b-1875-5d3f-b511-5ed29dd653e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372291} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc7a5ce-153b-5ee3-bff6-e9f8a921feec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372292} {:answer/id #uuid "3f065a62-494d-5897-98c5-77f62246f7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e905837-05cd-5930-b5d5-a8736fcd68df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372289} :answer-field/id #uuid "df0b3567-f146-5ada-98da-b9bb5e66153f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4820017f-1059-531a-a643-9f08294cdd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-140494" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed39a372-881a-5887-b603-d88e943dae07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372296} {:answer/id #uuid "b608f9a8-f0d6-5353-b03b-37080949693c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372297} {:answer/id #uuid "5764f478-5291-567a-85bd-08c0b7fbc141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372298} {:answer/id #uuid "866dcc81-ff13-5365-ae25-d0cda937abd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372299} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9da745-f68b-5ddc-8540-28551e3018d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "866dcc81-ff13-5365-ae25-d0cda937abd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372299} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a00122d-5c88-5cb5-b76e-9fceadb27bdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5605d4ea-f418-59f0-ade2-398cf6d0d3af" :question/math-academy-id "q-140496" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bb7c33-7d70-54c1-b928-ed8e10c801fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127401} {:answer/id #uuid "ab544de7-81aa-5555-8083-2ab360835387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127402} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6b8f11-ee8e-59c2-b126-7a746a53bb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127403} {:answer/id #uuid "4c091f46-17b3-5fd9-bed7-4e4049c71a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127404} {:answer/id #uuid "786de4d1-a3f6-5c49-8dc0-053bc311ab8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86bb7c33-7d70-54c1-b928-ed8e10c801fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127401} :answer-field/id #uuid "de1c1000-619a-54f4-bcb4-207769cbe877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31a3b63f-4053-528b-a2b8-f0ec571660f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38557" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6292f2cb-4fb3-5c84-9b96-954834eb5063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372303} {:answer/id #uuid "f6715ecf-bc9c-5b17-b358-ad6ad73c5323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372304} {:answer/id #uuid "16c8f0ff-aa17-5c60-a304-6bf5aa38e859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372305} {:answer/id #uuid "08998747-7d4f-57fa-bb73-d4f6d50f1676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372306} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e5fe18-cf22-54e6-8735-52a678dd27b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6292f2cb-4fb3-5c84-9b96-954834eb5063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372303} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8290e73-b03d-5954-ae27-64017cba770e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c1f1f01-a823-5b15-bbf5-e4ba72353ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-38562" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+2\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+2\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4+2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2296836-18b7-53d1-8414-1285ed5dd45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127413} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3f903b-cd0a-50de-98dd-aa8f1dec7f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127414} {:answer/id #uuid "ee52169d-ba3c-5566-a06d-23ad89855e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127415} {:answer/id #uuid "05b74c73-71da-5f93-95d1-0185e948ad1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127416} {:answer/id #uuid "35b27bd1-f7ed-5008-9fdb-871022568711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee52169d-ba3c-5566-a06d-23ad89855e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127415} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbec3dd-751d-527b-b37c-9f17e84ba1df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d6641ce-39fa-5304-84ca-9071211edd5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38565" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+3\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc13887b-9e01-5e43-990d-b826a9f00371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372310} {:answer/id #uuid "76b53110-5cad-58b2-a836-8778f7a8fdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372311} {:answer/id #uuid "c20c3c48-e46c-5dd2-a8d7-ab0ec78f1984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372312} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b71f87-7c40-5d22-a8fb-c9856e1a1ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372313} {:answer/id #uuid "bd85be3b-6e35-5286-8cee-3a6b6ae65508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc13887b-9e01-5e43-990d-b826a9f00371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372310} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dffe443-c84d-5b91-9e6e-279bccfe0190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5efb10ac-d6d7-5644-9104-60d88e699342" :question/math-academy-id "q-38569" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1+(-2)\\text{i}=1-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0+1\\text{i} \\\\ 2+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8034835a-2b0f-55d7-9ec4-415aaf887139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372317} {:answer/id #uuid "d801348a-ea00-5ee8-af5e-3f73c251bc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372318} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc7fb6a-2718-5fa2-a920-7fd77434323f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372319} {:answer/id #uuid "60514111-792d-5512-80b9-63bd27c452e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372320} {:answer/id #uuid "15eaa558-b6a6-5f09-aa8a-5bdceeb778c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60514111-792d-5512-80b9-63bd27c452e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372320} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aea677e-fcd9-56b4-b426-5d17e5429ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd091efb-cb45-5f15-b31b-c16f843dd2bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39127" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}-7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}5-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}-7\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "601c5b4f-aa64-55c4-a6a5-6a26212694fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372323} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab4d36d-51eb-5ae9-b884-89cd6e95119b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372324} {:answer/id #uuid "71fb3868-1a93-5eee-92e1-fd2dc082df1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1∓4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372325} {:answer/id #uuid "d37da79c-7573-55d4-bcb2-695a45ffcaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2\\pm 5\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372326} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1e865e-060a-5c10-9c52-04cd1ab5196c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\mp 2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1e865e-060a-5c10-9c52-04cd1ab5196c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5\\mp 2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372327} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09f017b-8560-5cc8-ae2d-be9293de13d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0d796fd-8159-54fb-b817-7bb654ebaf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-39130" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 5\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=5$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =5+(-2)\\text{i}=5-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7dad97-1327-54a0-a8ff-b71cc1d63ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2∓\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372330} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3ba9ef-4c36-5d07-9f5c-9adc51a30b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2∓7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372331} {:answer/id #uuid "16ee0f3c-1eb7-546f-8b92-d79de236abf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2∓7\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372332} {:answer/id #uuid "076eb016-4ca5-5424-89bf-07a8b39457ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372333} {:answer/id #uuid "3babe43c-1092-5eb5-8578-a16f32c03a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-7\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "076eb016-4ca5-5424-89bf-07a8b39457ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372333} :answer-field/id #uuid "1898eb85-a668-5b92-b3fc-b015f01b8423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f3fa040-fe21-5249-b083-19346af531bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39132" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & -7\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-7$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-7+(-2)\\text{i}=-7-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+(-2)\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6611222f-9dae-5ebb-94b2-89c731520f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372337} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce461d8-d8e1-55c1-92ef-deae216d72ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372338} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf6d7fc-3930-59e7-a472-8eb4d94d459e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372339} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca2c3bb-f761-531a-85b6-efb9dab85f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372340} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2c36b-6142-5b8b-83e0-433ceedecb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ce461d8-d8e1-55c1-92ef-deae216d72ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372338} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd42e1d-6d56-5f3b-b165-61f58e870e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00edf80e-08fc-5c39-9cfb-f07f06d93758" :question/math-academy-id "q-39143" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3+2\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3+2\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}+(-6-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a403cc2-ec31-5d64-acda-5f35916aa08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372344} {:answer/id #uuid "77b76cb9-af70-54fa-a0d2-8f8d30962c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2∓5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372345} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa8a653-056f-5cd0-a7ca-cb84abfe2e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372346} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9e9a3d-a2bf-5a6e-b7e4-d2a1b0188b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372347} {:answer/id #uuid "0702507c-bb35-5a55-bcb0-f8398a9f7dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9e9a3d-a2bf-5a6e-b7e4-d2a1b0188b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372347} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9004316-10a7-52ae-82fa-af5310d09883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fff68dac-09ab-5767-b8f2-36e8412fe6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99313" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -(-1) & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-1.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2+(-1)\\text{i}=-2-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3+0\\text{i} \\\\ 5+(-2)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bedcedd-1148-504a-a326-d1556153fc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2∓\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372351} {:answer/id #uuid "51f849db-6ba0-5a88-88a1-4d0fb6ebb052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372352} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c2b18f-d441-5553-9706-d9c6d74b13e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2∓\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372353} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5b1b1f-3ac6-5659-91a2-8955a242896e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372354} {:answer/id #uuid "a684839d-472a-576e-9042-3d04f501c9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4∓\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc5b1b1f-3ac6-5659-91a2-8955a242896e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5555fea-eff8-5079-b310-21780238d4a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0162928-bad4-5ebf-ba95-7b23bcc11874" :question/math-academy-id "q-99329" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0+(-2)\\text{i}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4+1\\text{i} \\\\ -3+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+\\text{i} \\\\ -3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ac5d4c-797f-563a-ac79-27e6e565e02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372358} {:answer/id #uuid "98fdd609-769d-5859-a88b-961cd42e7c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372359} {:answer/id #uuid "15cd1d4e-5d40-556f-8d2f-4280f312d7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372360} {:answer/id #uuid "08f110f7-97c9-5364-aaed-82fc74a0d960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372361} {:answer/id #uuid "786969b8-97b7-5a43-a703-4f48e15b9998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15cd1d4e-5d40-556f-8d2f-4280f312d7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372360} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee0d4e1-560e-5fe7-874e-d4c23c425814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cb45241-4b10-5c8f-9bf0-52f914e1a848" :question/math-academy-id "q-99337" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}7-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}7-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7dd41f-5966-55ea-964e-b9630564321e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372365} {:answer/id #uuid "37531b3e-157d-50f2-b954-b4b2af7f4941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372366} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac0ccca-1314-5dc4-878f-5f599ed6f1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372367} {:answer/id #uuid "fca1de61-27a7-5871-869e-53df31afc481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372368} {:answer/id #uuid "51e13629-63e4-5246-aa0a-30d654892624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac0ccca-1314-5dc4-878f-5f599ed6f1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372367} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd64e496-8198-5530-b3d6-715fea1b43d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41008973-043e-5aa2-862f-9bf9572530d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99411" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=4\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372d559f-9023-5573-9c56-f09e5bf7a2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372372} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbcd57f-d525-5ddf-8cd8-e2c8838f1a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372373} {:answer/id #uuid "ee204e12-a2a1-504a-b709-a26ce0ecc9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372374} {:answer/id #uuid "a785df69-e51c-5318-bab7-05a81432cde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372375} {:answer/id #uuid "83177c4b-4a5a-5818-b243-870ec0fba6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372d559f-9023-5573-9c56-f09e5bf7a2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372372} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9936c9-a23e-5a9e-ae7d-26cd5e1acd38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1be07fe-c7c3-5b64-8061-f1dc9ad8baf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99412" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=3+2\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e22acc-95ed-5062-bdaf-f132d09ec548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372379} {:answer/id #uuid "4f64cdbd-83d6-50f3-a8b1-1398ffd50f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372380} {:answer/id #uuid "7881da0c-1113-5235-9f30-8e327293c472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372381} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9b94bc-38f7-5d1b-97ff-9b91e9295eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372382} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c96893-a284-5dd5-bfed-520039e923e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7881da0c-1113-5235-9f30-8e327293c472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372381} :answer-field/id #uuid "37bbbc51-02da-57eb-9eca-78cee87f027a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80370013-119d-5754-8de4-7031d8d553b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99418" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-2+3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192a220e-01b6-5583-9b3a-2be04fc09da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372386} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1de990-7ace-519f-98fe-2c468d2ba1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372387} {:answer/id #uuid "4f628e76-1a2c-537c-8079-21f3e59558ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372388} {:answer/id #uuid "4bff9060-ab04-5c0a-a13c-753626b605b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372389} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7b2b4e-497c-5a58-909f-82fa48983721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc7b2b4e-497c-5a58-909f-82fa48983721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372390} :answer-field/id #uuid "35251ae5-b8d8-5b2a-80c6-754f748f841f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57e563fd-7adc-5039-9d3f-4d9ce896a93d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99419" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=7\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5c09a6-0645-566c-bd9b-b7f02f1b6f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372393} {:answer/id #uuid "38011547-feda-5a8c-9cca-7ffb2029f5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372394} {:answer/id #uuid "43aa2b39-220a-5429-892d-4e5b03fe4c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372395} {:answer/id #uuid "7d66e372-1f5a-5515-ac9e-ccb417d75a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372396} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2170d7-e6aa-513f-a145-cfb5fe29722e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43aa2b39-220a-5429-892d-4e5b03fe4c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372395} :answer-field/id #uuid "214b0415-595b-5843-a722-5f40eb8f45b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d4678f8-463f-58fd-85c7-59e31059248b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99428" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1+6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1-5\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41cdc071-bd1e-5ac8-99b4-21a8aff7b272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372400} {:answer/id #uuid "a648820e-f3a5-5dba-a8e3-b6cffb9b816d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372401} {:answer/id #uuid "dc818493-75f2-5752-bb47-ceb5835fa483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372402} {:answer/id #uuid "da621899-e44d-5bb6-940f-6e215e921e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372403} {:answer/id #uuid "b77f7641-3773-5a78-a934-785b53a1d0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da621899-e44d-5bb6-940f-6e215e921e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372403} :answer-field/id #uuid "d41f6a72-5970-5cef-a749-881d72649e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb9302d7-9539-5a6b-969f-fec724275dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-99469" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(2-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26fd9e0-d882-5271-a423-c0fc4ce4ce56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372407} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6ce13d-afe4-5d19-ad1a-da2f57044c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372408} {:answer/id #uuid "152a5a4b-e570-5685-a4f8-764ec14745c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372409} {:answer/id #uuid "af00d39d-220b-5038-b0d9-28954b511111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372410} {:answer/id #uuid "eff3b69b-c309-5a26-8411-95439a3599ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a6ce13d-afe4-5d19-ad1a-da2f57044c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372408} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e773762-00d2-5913-a740-949a1355328c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "049b3a30-c66d-50f0-8523-4c22e52552d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99480" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1-2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -13 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -13{x}_{1}+(-3+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$"}]}
